1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,737
In 2003, in a Hampshire village shop, sub-postmistress Jo Hamilton watched her

2
00:00:04,737 --> 00:00:07,105
computer invent a debt in front of her.

3
00:00:07,405 --> 00:00:11,994
The screen said two thousand pounds was missing from her post-office till — money

4
00:00:11,994 --> 00:00:13,071
she had never seen.

5
00:00:13,371 --> 00:00:18,053
She phoned the helpline and followed its instructions — and while she was on the

6
00:00:18,053 --> 00:00:20,569
line, the missing two thousand became four.

7
00:00:20,869 --> 00:00:22,866
She remortgaged her house to pay it.

8
00:00:23,166 --> 00:00:27,764
Her employer prosecuted her anyway — for theft, in front of her whole village.

9
00:00:28,064 --> 00:00:30,061
But the money never existed.

10
00:00:30,361 --> 00:00:31,801
The computer was lying.

11
00:00:32,101 --> 00:00:36,224
The people who owned it knew — and proving that would take twenty-one years, and an

12
00:00:36,224 --> 00:00:37,813
Act of Parliament like no other.

13
00:00:39,613 --> 00:00:44,354
What happened in that village shop happened, in some form, to roughly a thousand

14
00:00:44,354 --> 00:00:49,214
people — prosecuted on the word of one machine, most by their own employer, across

15
00:00:49,214 --> 00:00:53,777
sixteen years, while warning after warning was put in writing and filed away.

16
00:00:54,077 --> 00:00:55,749
England, 1999.

17
00:00:56,049 --> 00:00:57,721
It begins with an upgrade.

18
00:00:59,521 --> 00:01:03,979
A sub-postmaster is not quite a shopkeeper and not quite a civil servant.

19
00:01:04,279 --> 00:01:08,581
In Britain, the post office counter usually lives inside somebody's shop — the

20
00:01:08,581 --> 00:01:13,214
newsagent, the grocer — and the person behind it is a private citizen who has bought

21
00:01:13,214 --> 00:01:17,793
the right to serve their village on behalf of a centuries-old national institution.

22
00:01:18,093 --> 00:01:20,229
They hand out pensions on Thursdays.

23
00:01:20,529 --> 00:01:22,340
They take in Christmas savings.

24
00:01:22,640 --> 00:01:25,798
They know which customer needs the forms read aloud to them.

25
00:01:26,098 --> 00:01:31,216
In thousands of villages, the sub-postmaster was the closest thing the community had

26
00:01:31,216 --> 00:01:34,689
to a bank, a notary, and a friend with an official stamp.

27
00:01:34,989 --> 00:01:39,890
The red sign over the door meant the same thing on every high street in the country:

28
00:01:39,890 --> 00:01:44,091
the state, in walking distance, with a familiar face behind the counter.

29
00:01:44,391 --> 00:01:47,456
People queued at nine for their pensions and stayed to talk.

30
00:01:47,756 --> 00:01:52,711
The sub-postmaster cashed their cheques, weighed their parcels, kept their

31
00:01:52,711 --> 00:01:53,515
confidences.

32
00:01:53,815 --> 00:01:57,252
It was a good life — modest, respected, permanent.

33
00:01:57,552 --> 00:02:00,153
The people in this story did not drift into it.

34
00:02:00,453 --> 00:02:04,902
They bought their way in, with their savings and their houses, because running a

35
00:02:04,902 --> 00:02:09,462
village post office was the safest thing a careful family could do with its money.

36
00:02:09,762 --> 00:02:11,387
People paid for that privilege.

37
00:02:11,687 --> 00:02:16,995
In May 1998, a man named Alan Bates and his partner Suzanne Sercombe put sixty-five

38
00:02:16,995 --> 00:02:22,239
thousand pounds of their own money into a small post office in Craig-y-Don, on the

39
00:02:22,239 --> 00:02:23,390
North Wales coast.

40
00:02:23,690 --> 00:02:29,523
He was a careful man — the kind who kept receipts, filed paperwork, backed up his

41
00:02:29,523 --> 00:02:30,099
backups.

42
00:02:30,399 --> 00:02:33,464
That detail will matter more than anything else in this film.

43
00:02:33,764 --> 00:02:38,093
In 1999, the Post Office began rolling out a new computer system to every branch

44
00:02:38,093 --> 00:02:39,337
counter in the country.

45
00:02:39,637 --> 00:02:44,438
It was called Horizon, built by the British firm ICL under its Japanese owner,

46
00:02:44,438 --> 00:02:49,609
Fujitsu, and it was described at the time as the biggest non-military IT contract in

47
00:02:49,609 --> 00:02:50,040
Europe.

48
00:02:50,340 --> 00:02:55,077
The terminals arrived the way the future usually does — as an instruction.

49
00:02:55,377 --> 00:02:59,966
The paper ledgers that sub-postmasters had balanced by hand were carried out, and

50
00:02:59,966 --> 00:03:03,876
from that day the branch's truth was whatever the screen said it was.

51
00:03:04,176 --> 00:03:05,848
Most branches got on with it.

52
00:03:06,148 --> 00:03:10,493
The machine was modern, the institution was centuries old, and nobody buys a village

53
00:03:10,493 --> 00:03:12,510
post office expecting to need a lawyer.

54
00:03:12,810 --> 00:03:15,225
By late 2001, Horizon was everywhere.

55
00:03:15,525 --> 00:03:19,902
And almost at once, in shop after shop, the same strange scene began to play out at

56
00:03:19,902 --> 00:03:20,587
closing time.

57
00:03:20,887 --> 00:03:23,209
A sub-postmistress cashes up.

58
00:03:23,509 --> 00:03:24,995
She counts the till twice.

59
00:03:25,295 --> 00:03:29,929
The cash in her hand and the number on the screen do not agree — the screen says

60
00:03:29,929 --> 00:03:30,914
money is missing.

61
00:03:31,214 --> 00:03:33,629
Not a rounding error: hundreds of pounds.

62
00:03:33,929 --> 00:03:38,619
Sometimes thousands, appearing overnight, between locking the door and unlocking it.

63
00:03:38,919 --> 00:03:44,186
In Craig-y-Don, Horizon arrived in October 2000; by December, Alan Bates was staring

64
00:03:44,186 --> 00:03:47,510
at an unexplained hole of around six thousand pounds.

65
00:03:47,810 --> 00:03:49,250
He did what everyone did.

66
00:03:49,550 --> 00:03:50,850
He rang the helpline.

67
00:03:51,150 --> 00:03:55,005
The search that came first played out behind thousands of those counters.

68
00:03:55,305 --> 00:03:56,698
You count the till again.

69
00:03:56,998 --> 00:03:58,298
You count the safe.

70
00:03:58,598 --> 00:04:03,685
You go back through the week's receipts line by line, hunting for your own mistake —

71
00:04:03,685 --> 00:04:07,561
because it must be your mistake; the alternative is unthinkable.

72
00:04:07,861 --> 00:04:12,471
Husbands and wives sat up past midnight with shoeboxes of paper, checking each

73
00:04:12,471 --> 00:04:16,313
other's arithmetic against a machine that would not show its own.

74
00:04:16,613 --> 00:04:18,146
The screen did not argue.

75
00:04:18,446 --> 00:04:20,118
It simply repeated the number.

76
00:04:20,418 --> 00:04:23,483
The helpline had an answer, and the answer never changed.

77
00:04:23,783 --> 00:04:25,641
No one else is having this problem.

78
00:04:25,941 --> 00:04:27,938
You are the only one.

79
00:04:28,238 --> 00:04:33,447
The inquiry that finally examined this scandal heard that sentence described by

80
00:04:33,447 --> 00:04:38,787
sub-postmaster after sub-postmaster — the same reassurance, delivered up and down

81
00:04:38,787 --> 00:04:44,325
the country, to hundreds of people at once, each of them alone in a dark shop with a

82
00:04:44,325 --> 00:04:46,303
number that could not be true.

83
00:04:46,603 --> 00:04:48,089
It was not a small lie.

84
00:04:48,389 --> 00:04:50,339
It was the load-bearing lie.

85
00:04:50,639 --> 00:04:54,958
If you are the only one, the machine is fine — and the problem is you.

86
00:04:55,258 --> 00:04:59,530
And here the trap closed, because of a clause most of them had never dwelt on.

87
00:04:59,830 --> 00:05:04,493
Under their contracts, sub-postmasters were personally liable for losses shown in

88
00:05:04,493 --> 00:05:05,356
their accounts.

89
00:05:05,656 --> 00:05:10,588
The court that later tore this system apart put it simply: Horizon offered no way

90
00:05:10,588 --> 00:05:13,876
for a sub-postmaster to dispute Horizon's own figures.

91
00:05:14,176 --> 00:05:19,174
The screen said the money was gone; the contract said the shortfall was yours; and

92
00:05:19,174 --> 00:05:21,978
the institution treated an illusion as a debt.

93
00:05:22,278 --> 00:05:23,485
So they paid.

94
00:05:23,785 --> 00:05:24,900
From savings.

95
00:05:25,200 --> 00:05:26,686
From remortgages.

96
00:05:26,986 --> 00:05:27,961
From their parents.

97
00:05:28,261 --> 00:05:32,703
And some of them — exhausted, frightened, assured they were alone — pressed the

98
00:05:32,703 --> 00:05:37,427
button that said the accounts balanced when they did not, just to keep trading until

99
00:05:37,427 --> 00:05:38,664
someone sorted it out.

100
00:05:38,964 --> 00:05:41,472
British law has a name for pressing that button.

101
00:05:41,772 --> 00:05:45,487
It is called false accounting, and it carries a criminal record.

102
00:05:45,787 --> 00:05:47,552
Alan Bates refused to press it.

103
00:05:47,852 --> 00:05:52,023
He refused to sign for phantom losses at all, and he sent the Post Office letter

104
00:05:52,023 --> 00:05:53,796
after letter saying precisely why.

105
00:05:54,096 --> 00:05:59,413
In November 2003, the Post Office terminated his contract without giving a reason —

106
00:05:59,413 --> 00:06:03,384
its own files, disclosed years later, called him unmanageable.

107
00:06:03,684 --> 00:06:07,028
He lost the shop and the sixty-five thousand pounds.

108
00:06:07,328 --> 00:06:11,618
What the Post Office did not know was that it had just fired the one man in Britain

109
00:06:11,618 --> 00:06:13,272
who would never, ever let it go.

110
00:06:15,072 --> 00:06:16,465
Then the letters changed.

111
00:06:16,765 --> 00:06:21,750
Because the Post Office was not only these people's employer, their landlord of last

112
00:06:21,750 --> 00:06:24,242
resort, and the author of their contracts.

113
00:06:24,542 --> 00:06:29,194
In England and Wales, any company may bring a private prosecution — and the Post

114
00:06:29,194 --> 00:06:31,229
Office did, on an industrial scale.

115
00:06:31,529 --> 00:06:36,178
When a shortfall appeared, the Post Office investigated the crime, and the Post

116
00:06:36,178 --> 00:06:40,120
Office decided the charge, and the Post Office prosecuted the case.

117
00:06:40,420 --> 00:06:43,392
Victim, detective, and prosecutor, in one body.

118
00:06:43,692 --> 00:06:46,618
The police were not called, because the police were not needed.

119
00:06:46,918 --> 00:06:53,017
Between 1999 and 2015, roughly seven hundred sub-postmasters were prosecuted by the

120
00:06:53,017 --> 00:06:58,528
Post Office itself — on average, about one a week, for a decade and a half.

121
00:06:58,828 --> 00:07:02,913
And keep one number running from here to the end of this film: the number of Post

122
00:07:02,913 --> 00:07:07,048
Office or Fujitsu employees convicted of anything for what you are about to watch.

123
00:07:07,348 --> 00:07:08,695
It never changes.

124
00:07:08,995 --> 00:07:13,210
The method was consistent enough that a court would later describe it in cold

125
00:07:13,210 --> 00:07:13,593
detail.

126
00:07:13,893 --> 00:07:19,134
Investigators would arrive, suspend the sub-postmaster on the spot, and interview

127
00:07:19,134 --> 00:07:24,504
them under caution — the Post Office's own staff, playing detective in the shop the

128
00:07:24,504 --> 00:07:25,410
suspect owned.

129
00:07:25,710 --> 00:07:29,239
The charge sheet carried two counts: theft, and false accounting.

130
00:07:29,539 --> 00:07:33,115
Theft was the hammer — it meant prison, headlines, ruin.

131
00:07:33,415 --> 00:07:38,000
And then, quietly, an offer: plead guilty to false accounting, pay back the

132
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:40,567
shortfall, and the theft charge goes away.

133
00:07:40,867 --> 00:07:45,250
Jo Hamilton learned how the lever worked when the Post Office said her little shop

134
00:07:45,250 --> 00:07:48,297
in South Warnborough was thirty-six thousand pounds down.

135
00:07:48,597 --> 00:07:53,195
Its own investigators had reported, in writing, that there was no evidence of theft.

136
00:07:53,495 --> 00:07:58,888
She was charged with theft anyway — and offered the trade: plead guilty to fourteen

137
00:07:58,888 --> 00:08:04,151
counts of false accounting, repay thirty-six thousand, six hundred and forty-four

138
00:08:04,151 --> 00:08:06,684
pounds, and the theft count disappears.

139
00:08:06,984 --> 00:08:11,425
She took it, the way almost everyone took it, because the alternative was a trial

140
00:08:11,425 --> 00:08:15,483
she could not afford to lose, in front of people she had served for years.

141
00:08:15,783 --> 00:08:19,591
A criminal record, for the crime of believing her employer's computer.

142
00:08:19,891 --> 00:08:24,933
The court's file preserves the Post Office's condition in its own words: a plea "on

143
00:08:24,933 --> 00:08:29,793
the basis that the loss was due to the computer not working properly will not be

144
00:08:29,793 --> 00:08:30,340
accepted.

145
00:08:30,640 --> 00:08:36,217
In the case of Noel Thomas — a Welsh postman of forty-two years' service who ran the

146
00:08:36,217 --> 00:08:41,728
counter at Gaerwen, on Anglesey — the record is even blunter: his guilty plea would

147
00:08:41,728 --> 00:08:47,173
be accepted only on the basis that he accepted that Horizon was working perfectly.

148
00:08:47,473 --> 00:08:51,281
You could have your freedom, if you agreed the machine was innocent.

149
00:08:51,581 --> 00:08:56,225
Thomas went to prison for a forty-eight-thousand-pound shortfall that never existed.

150
00:08:56,525 --> 00:08:58,058
He turned sixty inside.

151
00:08:58,358 --> 00:09:03,174
Around the same era, guidance issued to Post Office investigators was sorting the

152
00:09:03,174 --> 00:09:08,050
people they interviewed into racial categories — a document that would not surface

153
00:09:08,050 --> 00:09:09,596
for another fifteen years.

154
00:09:09,896 --> 00:09:12,264
Not everyone could be pushed to a plea.

155
00:09:12,564 --> 00:09:16,790
Lee Castleton ran the post office in Bridlington, on the Yorkshire coast.

156
00:09:17,090 --> 00:09:22,435
From January 2004 his screen conjured shortfall after shortfall — around twenty-five

157
00:09:22,435 --> 00:09:27,780
thousand pounds — and he refused to accept the debt was real, because he had counted

158
00:09:27,780 --> 00:09:28,607
his own till.

159
00:09:28,907 --> 00:09:33,654
So the Post Office took him to the High Court, in a civil suit, where there is no

160
00:09:33,654 --> 00:09:36,291
jury and the loser pays the winner's lawyers.

161
00:09:36,591 --> 00:09:41,944
Castleton had no money for barristers; he represented himself, a shopkeeper alone

162
00:09:41,944 --> 00:09:43,464
against an institution.

163
00:09:43,764 --> 00:09:44,832
He lost.

164
00:09:45,132 --> 00:09:49,416
And with the verdict came the bill for the Post Office's costs: three hundred and

165
00:09:49,416 --> 00:09:50,844
twenty-one thousand pounds.

166
00:09:51,144 --> 00:09:56,067
He was bankrupted — over a phantom debt one-thirteenth that size.

167
00:09:56,367 --> 00:09:59,339
His wife began having seizures brought on by stress.

168
00:09:59,639 --> 00:10:01,682
His children were bullied at school.

169
00:10:01,982 --> 00:10:03,422
The family lost everything.

170
00:10:03,722 --> 00:10:06,601
The Post Office never collected most of the money.

171
00:10:06,901 --> 00:10:08,480
Collection was not the point.

172
00:10:08,780 --> 00:10:13,052
The point was what happened to the next sub-postmaster who thought about fighting.

173
00:10:13,352 --> 00:10:15,070
And then there is Seema Misra.

174
00:10:15,370 --> 00:10:18,389
She ran the post office at West Byfleet, in Surrey.

175
00:10:18,689 --> 00:10:23,428
In January 2008 an audit found her branch seventy-nine thousand pounds short on

176
00:10:23,428 --> 00:10:24,448
Horizon's ledger.

177
00:10:24,748 --> 00:10:29,144
She had put in her own money; she had told the helpline again and again that the

178
00:10:29,144 --> 00:10:31,946
system was generating losses she could not explain.

179
00:10:32,246 --> 00:10:37,193
She was charged with theft — the sum on the indictment, seventy-four thousand, six

180
00:10:37,193 --> 00:10:42,200
hundred and nine pounds and eighty-four pence, calculated to the penny by a machine

181
00:10:42,200 --> 00:10:43,949
that could not do arithmetic.

182
00:10:44,249 --> 00:10:49,415
At Guildford Crown Court in October 2010, the Post Office's expert witness assured

183
00:10:49,415 --> 00:10:51,494
the jury the system was reliable.

184
00:10:51,794 --> 00:10:52,955
She was convicted.

185
00:10:53,255 --> 00:10:58,538
On the eleventh of November 2010 — her son's tenth birthday — Seema Misra was sent

186
00:10:58,538 --> 00:11:00,407
to prison for fifteen months.

187
00:11:00,707 --> 00:11:02,332
She was eight weeks pregnant.

188
00:11:02,632 --> 00:11:07,239
She served her time at Bronzefield, and when her second son was born the following

189
00:11:07,239 --> 00:11:09,598
summer, she was wearing an electronic tag.

190
00:11:09,898 --> 00:11:12,777
A pregnant woman went to prison on the word of a computer.

191
00:11:13,077 --> 00:11:16,653
That sentence should be impossible to write about modern Britain.

192
00:11:16,953 --> 00:11:21,695
Now here is the one that is worse: eleven weeks before her jury came back, the Post

193
00:11:21,695 --> 00:11:26,437
Office had already put in writing what would happen if anyone looked too closely at

194
00:11:26,437 --> 00:11:27,123
the machine.

195
00:11:28,923 --> 00:11:30,130
August 2010.

196
00:11:30,430 --> 00:11:34,830
An internal report lands on senior desks at the Post Office, written by the head of

197
00:11:34,830 --> 00:11:36,421
product and branch accounting.

198
00:11:36,721 --> 00:11:40,622
Its subject: how to respond to challenges to Horizon's integrity.

199
00:11:40,922 --> 00:11:45,796
Its logic, in the Post Office's own preserved words, is this: any independent

200
00:11:45,796 --> 00:11:50,480
investigation of the system would have to be disclosed in court — and "any

201
00:11:50,480 --> 00:11:55,797
perception that POL doubts its own systems would mean that all criminal prosecutions

202
00:11:55,797 --> 00:11:57,315
would have to be stayed.

203
00:11:57,615 --> 00:12:01,518
It would also beg a question for the Court of Appeal over past prosecutions and

204
00:12:01,518 --> 00:12:02,259
imprisonments."

205
00:12:02,559 --> 00:12:05,113
There it is, in one paragraph, in 2010.

206
00:12:05,413 --> 00:12:08,710
Check the machine honestly, and every prosecution stops.

207
00:12:09,010 --> 00:12:12,957
Check the machine honestly, and the ones already in prison get to appeal.

208
00:12:13,257 --> 00:12:18,323
The institution had written down the price of the truth — and decided, prosecution

209
00:12:18,323 --> 00:12:20,177
by prosecution, not to pay it.

210
00:12:20,477 --> 00:12:23,635
Seema Misra was convicted eleven weeks after that report.

211
00:12:23,935 --> 00:12:28,658
The expert who vouched for Horizon at her trial was a Fujitsu engineer named Gareth

212
00:12:28,658 --> 00:12:33,267
Jenkins, whose standard assurance was that the system "will accurately record all

213
00:12:33,267 --> 00:12:36,567
data that is submitted to it and correctly account for it.

214
00:12:36,867 --> 00:12:40,966
The Court of Appeal would later record he had known of live Horizon bugs since

215
00:12:40,966 --> 00:12:44,855
September of that same year — and said nothing about them in his evidence.

216
00:12:45,155 --> 00:12:48,870
Because the bugs were real, and inside Fujitsu they had names.

217
00:12:49,170 --> 00:12:53,682
The Callendar Square bug, known about since the mid-2000s, which duplicated

218
00:12:53,682 --> 00:12:54,464
transactions.

219
00:12:54,764 --> 00:12:59,192
The receipts-and-payments mismatch bug of 2010 — an internal note records

220
00:12:59,192 --> 00:13:04,105
discrepancies that vanish from the counter screen "but will still show within the

221
00:13:04,105 --> 00:13:08,835
back end branch account," quietly corrupting the books of some forty branches.

222
00:13:09,135 --> 00:13:11,318
Their existence was not folklore.

223
00:13:11,618 --> 00:13:15,963
It was ticketed, logged, and discussed on the vendor's side of a wall the

224
00:13:15,963 --> 00:13:17,748
sub-postmasters could not see.

225
00:13:18,048 --> 00:13:22,657
And on the far side of that wall sat something even the bugs could not match for

226
00:13:22,657 --> 00:13:27,324
secrecy: a support centre in Bracknell where Fujitsu engineers could reach into a

227
00:13:27,324 --> 00:13:31,876
branch's live accounts and change the numbers — without the sub-postmaster ever

228
00:13:31,876 --> 00:13:33,605
knowing anyone had been there.

229
00:13:33,905 --> 00:13:38,735
For years, the Post Office's public position was that this was flatly impossible.

230
00:13:39,035 --> 00:13:44,347
In 2012, under pressure from MPs led by James Arbuthnot, the Post Office appointed

231
00:13:44,347 --> 00:13:49,595
independent investigators — a small forensic accountancy firm, Second Sight — and

232
00:13:49,595 --> 00:13:51,992
for a moment it looked like daylight.

233
00:13:52,292 --> 00:13:57,317
Their first report, in July 2013, confirmed two live defects and a support system

234
00:13:57,317 --> 00:14:00,233
that failed the very people it existed to help.

235
00:14:00,533 --> 00:14:05,522
That same month, the Post Office's own barrister, Simon Clarke, delivered a

236
00:14:05,522 --> 00:14:11,110
devastating legal advice: the expert evidence underpinning years of prosecutions was

237
00:14:11,110 --> 00:14:14,836
unreliable, because the expert had known about the bugs.

238
00:14:15,136 --> 00:14:17,876
Weeks later, Clarke recorded something darker.

239
00:14:18,176 --> 00:14:22,115
The Post Office had set up weekly calls to track Horizon's defects — and then

240
00:14:22,115 --> 00:14:25,235
someone gave an instruction about the minutes of those calls.

241
00:14:25,535 --> 00:14:30,662
The advice preserves it in one sentence: the emails and minutes "should be, and have

242
00:14:30,662 --> 00:14:34,080
been, destroyed: the word 'shredded' was conveyed to me.

243
00:14:34,380 --> 00:14:39,464
The thinking, as recorded, was simpler still: if it's not minuted, it's not

244
00:14:39,464 --> 00:14:40,278
disclosable.

245
00:14:40,578 --> 00:14:45,748
That summer — the summer its own lawyer used the word shredded — the Post Office was

246
00:14:45,748 --> 00:14:48,333
wearing down a man named Martin Griffiths.

247
00:14:48,633 --> 00:14:53,370
He had run the post office on Hope Farm Road in Ellesmere Port since 1995.

248
00:14:53,670 --> 00:14:58,647
From 2009, the shortfalls came, and the helpline told him what it told everyone —

249
00:14:58,647 --> 00:15:03,562
his family say he was made to feel he was the only one, while the same complaint

250
00:15:03,562 --> 00:15:05,651
poured in from across the country.

251
00:15:05,951 --> 00:15:10,710
He repaid and repaid; his elderly parents put in their life savings, about sixty-two

252
00:15:10,710 --> 00:15:11,617
thousand pounds.

253
00:15:11,917 --> 00:15:16,706
In May 2013 his branch was robbed, and the Post Office's investigator held him

254
00:15:16,706 --> 00:15:19,162
partly responsible for the stolen money.

255
00:15:19,462 --> 00:15:22,666
In July 2013, it gave him notice of termination.

256
00:15:22,966 --> 00:15:27,998
On the twenty-third of September 2013, Martin Griffiths deliberately walked in front

257
00:15:27,998 --> 00:15:28,957
of a moving bus.

258
00:15:29,257 --> 00:15:31,625
He died in hospital eighteen days later.

259
00:15:31,925 --> 00:15:33,179
He was fifty-nine.

260
00:15:33,479 --> 00:15:37,380
The Post Office later reached a confidential settlement with his family.

261
00:15:37,680 --> 00:15:41,664
The public inquiry would eventually be told of at least thirteen people who took

262
00:15:41,664 --> 00:15:45,847
their own lives in connection with this scandal, and of at least fifty-nine more who

263
00:15:45,847 --> 00:15:46,643
contemplated it.

264
00:15:46,943 --> 00:15:50,101
Watch what the institution did next, because it is the tell.

265
00:15:50,401 --> 00:15:56,004
The prosecutions quietly stopped — from forty-two in a single year to two, then zero

266
00:15:56,004 --> 00:16:01,473
— with no announcement, no review of the convictions already won, no letter to the

267
00:16:01,473 --> 00:16:02,940
people already ruined.

268
00:16:03,240 --> 00:16:08,842
In March 2015, the Post Office sacked Second Sight and, as Arbuthnot told the House

269
00:16:08,842 --> 00:16:12,621
of Commons that week, told it to destroy all its papers.

270
00:16:12,921 --> 00:16:17,909
In January 2015, preparing to face a committee of MPs on the question of remote

271
00:16:17,909 --> 00:16:23,023
access, chief executive Paula Vennells wrote in an email disclosed years later to

272
00:16:23,023 --> 00:16:26,621
the public inquiry: "I need to say no it is not possible.

273
00:16:26,921 --> 00:16:31,449
A High Court judgment would later quote what the chief executive's office was asking

274
00:16:31,449 --> 00:16:34,630
behind closed doors that January: "What is the true answer?

275
00:16:34,930 --> 00:16:38,088
I hope it is that we know this is not possible.

276
00:16:38,388 --> 00:16:43,335
Its formal answer to Parliament that same winter was absolute: there is "no

277
00:16:43,335 --> 00:16:48,413
functionality in Horizon for either a branch, Post Office or Fujitsu to edit,

278
00:16:48,413 --> 00:16:53,491
manipulate or remove transaction data once it has been recorded in a branch's

279
00:16:53,491 --> 00:16:54,085
accounts.

280
00:16:54,385 --> 00:16:59,193
And when the BBC's Panorama put the question directly that summer, with a former

281
00:16:59,193 --> 00:17:04,061
Fujitsu engineer named Richard Roll saying on camera that engineers could and did

282
00:17:04,061 --> 00:17:07,667
reach into branch accounts, the Post Office denied it again.

283
00:17:07,967 --> 00:17:10,661
Every part of that sentence would die in a courtroom.

284
00:17:10,961 --> 00:17:14,676
It just needed someone with twenty years of patience to carry it there.

285
00:17:16,476 --> 00:17:18,844
The army had been forming since 2009.

286
00:17:19,144 --> 00:17:24,620
In May of that year, a trade magazine — Computer Weekly — had published the first

287
00:17:24,620 --> 00:17:29,826
story anyone dared to print, by a young reporter named Rebecca Thomson: seven

288
00:17:29,826 --> 00:17:33,680
sub-postmasters, seven impossible shortfalls, one system.

289
00:17:33,980 --> 00:17:36,209
Alan Bates was one of the seven.

290
00:17:36,509 --> 00:17:40,999
That November, he hired a village hall in Fenny Compton, Warwickshire — picked more

291
00:17:40,999 --> 00:17:45,326
or less at random, because it sat in the middle of England — and invited whoever

292
00:17:45,326 --> 00:17:46,354
else was out there.

293
00:17:46,654 --> 00:17:48,930
A couple of dozen strangers walked in.

294
00:17:49,230 --> 00:17:50,530
They had never met.

295
00:17:50,830 --> 00:17:54,917
Each of them had been told, for years, that they were the only one.

296
00:17:55,217 --> 00:17:59,718
They looked around a room full of only-ones, and they gave themselves a name: the

297
00:17:59,718 --> 00:18:01,719
Justice for Subpostmasters Alliance.

298
00:18:02,019 --> 00:18:05,502
The press lane stayed narrow, but it never quite closed.

299
00:18:05,802 --> 00:18:10,937
In November 2010 a radio reporter named Nick Wallis took a call from a man whose

300
00:18:10,937 --> 00:18:16,008
pregnant wife had just been sent to prison over a till that would not balance —

301
00:18:16,008 --> 00:18:18,062
Seema Misra's husband, Davinder.

302
00:18:18,362 --> 00:18:23,298
Wallis spent the next decade on the story when almost nobody else would touch it, on

303
00:18:23,298 --> 00:18:27,000
regional television, then in Private Eye, eventually in a book.

304
00:18:27,300 --> 00:18:30,411
The facts were available for years to anyone who looked.

305
00:18:30,711 --> 00:18:32,104
Almost nobody looked.

306
00:18:32,404 --> 00:18:37,323
What followed was not a montage; it was a grind that consumed the better part of two

307
00:18:37,323 --> 00:18:37,791
decades.

308
00:18:38,091 --> 00:18:43,583
Bates ran the campaign the way he had run his branch — from a kitchen table, on a

309
00:18:43,583 --> 00:18:44,871
laptop, with files.

310
00:18:45,171 --> 00:18:47,679
He wrote to ministers and was patted on the head.

311
00:18:47,979 --> 00:18:50,905
He gathered members in the dozens, then the hundreds.

312
00:18:51,205 --> 00:18:56,552
And in 2017, five hundred and fifty-five sub-postmasters, bankrolled by litigation

313
00:18:56,552 --> 00:19:01,964
funders because none of them had money left, sued the Post Office in the High Court

314
00:19:01,964 --> 00:19:03,790
under a single group action.

315
00:19:04,090 --> 00:19:09,227
The lead name on the claim, alphabetically and in every other sense: Bates versus

316
00:19:09,227 --> 00:19:09,988
Post Office.

317
00:19:10,288 --> 00:19:12,842
The institution fought like an institution.

318
00:19:13,142 --> 00:19:15,928
It ran up costs designed to outlast the funders.

319
00:19:16,228 --> 00:19:21,731
In March 2019, when the first judgment savaged its sub-postmaster contracts, it did

320
00:19:21,731 --> 00:19:27,168
something almost unheard of: mid-trial, it applied to remove the judge, Mr Justice

321
00:19:27,168 --> 00:19:28,627
Fraser, from the case.

322
00:19:28,927 --> 00:19:32,874
The application failed within weeks, and the appeal court refused to touch it.

323
00:19:33,174 --> 00:19:37,502
And in December 2019, Fraser handed down the judgment the Post Office had spent

324
00:19:37,502 --> 00:19:40,186
twenty years making sure no one would ever write.

325
00:19:40,486 --> 00:19:45,044
The shoeboxes of receipts and the backed-up backups had finally met a judge who read

326
00:19:45,044 --> 00:19:45,641
every page.

327
00:19:45,941 --> 00:19:49,192
Legacy Horizon, he found, was "not remotely robust.

328
00:19:49,492 --> 00:19:54,437
The Post Office's approach to the mounting evidence, he wrote, "demonstrates a

329
00:19:54,437 --> 00:19:59,636
simple institutional obstinacy or refusal to consider any possible alternatives to

330
00:19:59,636 --> 00:20:04,835
their view of Horizon" — its insistence on the system's integrity amounted to "the

331
00:20:04,835 --> 00:20:08,765
21st century equivalent of maintaining that the earth is flat.

332
00:20:09,065 --> 00:20:11,944
And the denials about Bracknell collapsed entirely.

333
00:20:12,244 --> 00:20:17,492
Remote access to branch accounts, the court found, "does exist; such remote access

334
00:20:17,492 --> 00:20:22,740
is possible by employees within Fujitsu; it does exist specifically by design; and

335
00:20:22,740 --> 00:20:24,597
it has been used in the past.

336
00:20:24,897 --> 00:20:29,511
The Post Office's years of public assurances were, in the judge's words,

337
00:20:29,511 --> 00:20:31,306
"specifically wrong in fact.

338
00:20:31,606 --> 00:20:35,742
Before Christmas, Fraser referred his concerns about the evidence of Fujitsu's

339
00:20:35,742 --> 00:20:38,340
witnesses to the Director of Public Prosecutions.

340
00:20:38,640 --> 00:20:40,730
The 555 had won.

341
00:20:41,030 --> 00:20:42,841
Now look at what winning bought them.

342
00:20:43,141 --> 00:20:48,647
The Post Office had in fact already seen enough: days before the judgment came down,

343
00:20:48,647 --> 00:20:52,383
it settled — fifty-seven point seven five million pounds.

344
00:20:52,683 --> 00:20:57,118
And after the litigation funders and the lawyers who had made the case possible took

345
00:20:57,118 --> 00:21:01,553
their share, the sub-postmasters were left with roughly twelve million between them.

346
00:21:01,853 --> 00:21:06,358
About twenty thousand pounds each, for ruined businesses, bankruptcies, and prison.

347
00:21:06,658 --> 00:21:10,915
Alan Bates had beaten the Post Office in open court and could not even cover his

348
00:21:10,915 --> 00:21:11,766
members' losses.

349
00:21:12,066 --> 00:21:14,713
But the judgment was never really about the money.

350
00:21:15,013 --> 00:21:16,081
It was a key.

351
00:21:16,381 --> 00:21:21,487
Criminal convictions built on a machine now judicially certified as unreliable could

352
00:21:21,487 --> 00:21:26,532
finally be appealed — and the Criminal Cases Review Commission began referring them

353
00:21:26,532 --> 00:21:27,201
in batches.

354
00:21:27,501 --> 00:21:32,099
In December 2020, the first six convictions fell at Southwark Crown Court.

355
00:21:32,399 --> 00:21:37,373
And on the twenty-third of April 2021, at the Royal Courts of Justice, the Court of

356
00:21:37,373 --> 00:21:41,269
Appeal ruled on thirty-nine more — and did not merely quash them.

357
00:21:41,569 --> 00:21:43,566
It found the Post Office had known.

358
00:21:43,866 --> 00:21:47,210
"POL knew that there were problems with Horizon.

359
00:21:47,510 --> 00:21:52,525
POL knew that SPMs around the country had complained of inexplicable discrepancies…

360
00:21:52,525 --> 00:21:57,541
In short, POL knew that there were serious issues about the reliability of Horizon.

361
00:21:57,841 --> 00:22:02,450
It had, the court said, "effectively steamrolled over any SPM who sought to

362
00:22:02,450 --> 00:22:07,121
challenge its accuracy" — and its failures were "so egregious as to make the

363
00:22:07,121 --> 00:22:12,284
prosecution of any of the 'Horizon cases' an affront to the conscience of the court.

364
00:22:12,584 --> 00:22:17,292
On the pavement outside stood Jo Hamilton, eighteen years after a helpline talked

365
00:22:17,292 --> 00:22:21,942
her debt from two thousand pounds to four — no longer, in the eyes of the law, a

366
00:22:21,942 --> 00:22:22,290
thief.

367
00:22:22,590 --> 00:22:26,770
Cameras caught her half-laughing, half-crying, holding the paperwork.

368
00:22:27,070 --> 00:22:28,556
Thirty-nine names cleared.

369
00:22:28,856 --> 00:22:34,429
Then more, in batches through 2022 and 2023 — about a hundred in all.

370
00:22:34,729 --> 00:22:39,916
Which left roughly nine hundred convictions still standing, held by people now old,

371
00:22:39,916 --> 00:22:44,853
or ill, or dead, many of whom would not go near a courtroom again for anything.

372
00:22:45,153 --> 00:22:49,333
At the pace the system was managing, the last of them would die convicted.

373
00:22:49,633 --> 00:22:52,744
That is where this story stood on New Year's Eve, 2023.

374
00:22:53,044 --> 00:22:56,573
What broke the logjam was not a judgment, and not a report.

375
00:22:56,873 --> 00:22:58,591
It was a television schedule.

376
00:23:00,391 --> 00:23:06,211
On the first of January 2024, ITV began a four-night drama called Mr Bates vs the

377
00:23:06,211 --> 00:23:11,672
Post Office — actors playing Bates, Hamilton, Misra, the helpline calls, the

378
00:23:11,672 --> 00:23:14,834
shredded minutes, the whole quarter-century.

379
00:23:15,134 --> 00:23:16,620
Nothing in it was new.

380
00:23:16,920 --> 00:23:21,682
Every fact had been in the public record for years, in judgments almost nobody

381
00:23:21,682 --> 00:23:23,514
outside the campaign had read.

382
00:23:23,814 --> 00:23:28,689
But close to ten million people watched it in a week — thirteen and a half million

383
00:23:28,689 --> 00:23:33,445
by spring — and Britain went from not knowing the story to being unable to think

384
00:23:33,445 --> 00:23:34,634
about anything else.

385
00:23:34,934 --> 00:23:40,451
Within days, more than 1.2 million people signed a petition demanding Paula Vennells

386
00:23:40,451 --> 00:23:45,574
lose her CBE — the honour she had been given in 2019, for services to the Post

387
00:23:45,574 --> 00:23:46,033
Office.

388
00:23:46,333 --> 00:23:50,931
On the ninth of January she announced: "I return my CBE with immediate effect.

389
00:23:51,231 --> 00:23:55,155
On the tenth, the Prime Minister stood up in Parliament and promised something

390
00:23:55,155 --> 00:23:56,061
without precedent.

391
00:23:56,361 --> 00:24:01,013
The running number — employees of the Post Office or Fujitsu convicted for any of

392
00:24:01,013 --> 00:24:03,884
this — stood at zero on the night the drama aired.

393
00:24:04,184 --> 00:24:07,388
It is worth watching closely through the reckoning that follows.

394
00:24:07,688 --> 00:24:12,555
On the twenty-fourth of May 2024, the Post Office (Horizon System) Offences Act

395
00:24:12,555 --> 00:24:13,911
received royal assent.

396
00:24:14,211 --> 00:24:19,111
Its first sentence does something no British law had ever done at this scale: "Every

397
00:24:19,111 --> 00:24:23,777
conviction to which this Act applies is quashed on the coming into force of this

398
00:24:23,777 --> 00:24:24,010
Act.

399
00:24:24,310 --> 00:24:25,378
Not reviewed.

400
00:24:25,678 --> 00:24:26,793
Not referred.

401
00:24:27,093 --> 00:24:32,135
Quashed — hundreds of convictions, at the stroke of the King's pen, most of them

402
00:24:32,135 --> 00:24:35,917
without any court ever looking at the individual case again.

403
00:24:36,217 --> 00:24:40,556
In England and Wales alone, six hundred and nineteen people have so far been

404
00:24:40,556 --> 00:24:42,440
identified as cleared by the Act.

405
00:24:42,740 --> 00:24:47,279
Parliament had already, back in January, rushed through a first Act to get money

406
00:24:47,279 --> 00:24:51,988
moving; the cleared were offered six hundred thousand pounds each, full and final —

407
00:24:51,988 --> 00:24:55,790
or a longer, harder road if they believed their losses were bigger.

408
00:24:56,090 --> 00:25:01,216
The country's most senior former judges rose in the House of Lords to object, and

409
00:25:01,216 --> 00:25:06,342
their objection measures the failure: Parliament does not quash convictions, said

410
00:25:06,342 --> 00:25:10,393
the former Lord Chief Justice — that is a matter for the courts.

411
00:25:10,693 --> 00:25:11,715
He was right.

412
00:25:12,015 --> 00:25:16,515
That is exactly how bad it had become: the miscarriage was too big for the courts

413
00:25:16,515 --> 00:25:21,071
that caused it, and the constitution had to be bent to clear the innocent in bulk.

414
00:25:21,371 --> 00:25:24,204
The calendar of that week is its own verdict.

415
00:25:24,504 --> 00:25:29,862
On the very Friday the Act received the King's assent, across London, Paula Vennells

416
00:25:29,862 --> 00:25:35,029
was completing three days of evidence to the public inquiry — under a barrister's

417
00:25:35,029 --> 00:25:40,323
questions about the email that said I need to say no, about what she had been told,

418
00:25:40,323 --> 00:25:40,897
and when.

419
00:25:41,197 --> 00:25:42,637
She wept repeatedly.

420
00:25:42,937 --> 00:25:45,398
"I was too trusting," she told the inquiry.

421
00:25:45,698 --> 00:25:50,405
Three months earlier, the King had formally erased her honour; the notice in the

422
00:25:50,405 --> 00:25:54,289
official Gazette records that her CBE was "cancelled and annulled.

423
00:25:54,589 --> 00:25:59,000
It remains, to date, one of the very few individual consequences anyone connected to

424
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:00,208
this scandal has faced.

425
00:26:00,508 --> 00:26:06,144
In July 2025, the inquiry's chairman, Sir Wyn Williams, published the first volume

426
00:26:06,144 --> 00:26:09,099
of his final report — the human accounting.

427
00:26:09,399 --> 00:26:13,764
Roughly one thousand people prosecuted and convicted on Horizon evidence.

428
00:26:14,064 --> 00:26:17,222
About ten thousand people now eligible for redress.

429
00:26:17,522 --> 00:26:22,027
At least thirteen suicides linked to the scandal, a number he said may be higher.

430
00:26:22,327 --> 00:26:27,562
His word for the impact was "disastrous," and his finding was that these people were

431
00:26:27,562 --> 00:26:32,734
victims of "wholly unacceptable behaviour" — by individuals, and by the Post Office

432
00:26:32,734 --> 00:26:34,541
and Fujitsu "as institutions.

433
00:26:34,841 --> 00:26:39,271
He attached nineteen recommendations, and a deadline for the institutions to answer

434
00:26:39,271 --> 00:26:39,485
him.

435
00:26:39,785 --> 00:26:44,801
By the summer of 2026, the government's ledger showed about one point six billion

436
00:26:44,801 --> 00:26:49,940
pounds paid out to more than twelve thousand nine hundred claimants — real money at

437
00:26:49,940 --> 00:26:55,079
last, though Sir Alan Bates, knighted in 2024, was by then writing that the schemes

438
00:26:55,079 --> 00:27:00,218
had been turned into "quasi-kangaroo courts" — his own offer less than half of what

439
00:27:00,218 --> 00:27:00,962
he had lost.

440
00:27:01,262 --> 00:27:06,649
Fujitsu told Parliament in January 2024 it had a "moral obligation" to contribute.

441
00:27:06,949 --> 00:27:12,545
As of the middle of 2026, it had contributed nothing — and its engineers still run

442
00:27:12,545 --> 00:27:18,278
Horizon, on a contract extended to 2027, because the machine at the centre of all of

443
00:27:18,278 --> 00:27:22,646
this is still, today, on the counters of Britain's post offices.

444
00:27:24,446 --> 00:27:25,746
So finish the count.

445
00:27:26,046 --> 00:27:29,668
Roughly one thousand people convicted on the word of a broken machine.

446
00:27:29,968 --> 00:27:34,732
About seven hundred of them prosecuted by their own employer — one a week, for

447
00:27:34,732 --> 00:27:35,587
fifteen years.

448
00:27:35,887 --> 00:27:39,509
By the BBC's count, two hundred and thirty-six of them went to prison.

449
00:27:39,809 --> 00:27:42,874
At least thirteen people are dead by their own hand.

450
00:27:43,174 --> 00:27:48,407
And the number of human beings convicted of any crime for doing this to them — after

451
00:27:48,407 --> 00:27:53,266
the shredded minutes, the buried advice, the denials a High Court judge called

452
00:27:53,266 --> 00:27:55,945
specifically wrong in fact — is still zero.

453
00:27:56,245 --> 00:28:01,390
A police investigation with more than a hundred officers has fifty-three people

454
00:28:01,390 --> 00:28:06,600
under examination; it does not expect to put files before prosecutors until late

455
00:28:06,600 --> 00:28:06,926
2027.

456
00:28:07,226 --> 00:28:11,766
The inquiry volumes that will name the culpable are, as of this summer, still

457
00:28:11,766 --> 00:28:12,474
unpublished.

458
00:28:12,774 --> 00:28:17,903
The detective leading the criminal case said it plainly this spring: many victims

459
00:28:17,903 --> 00:28:22,526
are old now, some are already gone — "we do not have the luxury of time."

460
00:28:22,826 --> 00:28:27,226
Somewhere in England tomorrow morning, a shutter will go up over a post-office

461
00:28:27,226 --> 00:28:30,442
counter, and the screen behind it will still say Horizon.

462
00:28:30,742 --> 00:28:35,823
The system was told the truth about itself in 2010, in writing, and calculated that

463
00:28:35,823 --> 00:28:37,476
the truth was unaffordable.

464
00:28:37,776 --> 00:28:42,274
It spent the next decade buying time with other people's lives — and the bill, so

465
00:28:42,274 --> 00:28:45,717
far, has been paid by everyone except the people who wrote it.

466
00:28:46,017 --> 00:28:48,571
One more line of the ledger deserves its place.

467
00:28:48,871 --> 00:28:54,198
For years, a helpline told frightened people, one call at a time, that they were the

468
00:28:54,198 --> 00:28:54,769
only one.

469
00:28:55,069 --> 00:28:57,437
There turned out to be ten thousand of them.

470
00:28:57,737 --> 00:29:02,776
The sentence was a lie the first time it was spoken and a lie every time after — and

471
00:29:02,776 --> 00:29:06,375
the cure for it, in the end, was not a court, and not a law.

472
00:29:06,675 --> 00:29:11,198
It was a village hall in the middle of England, and a handful of ruined shopkeepers

473
00:29:11,198 --> 00:29:13,595
discovering that the only-ones were an army.

474
00:29:13,895 --> 00:29:18,320
If you think a machine's word should never outweigh a human being's — and that

475
00:29:18,320 --> 00:29:22,802
someone, eventually, must answer for the ones it buried — hit like, so the next

476
00:29:22,802 --> 00:29:25,923
story like this gets told before it takes twenty years.
